(ns net.b12n.lambda-mvp.main
  "Entry point -- compiled to a native binary by `jank compile`.
  A container's ENTRYPOINT execs this binary directly.

  NO try/catch here, unlike the Jolt version's main.clj -- try/catch
  is verified prohibitively slow to jank compile (15+ minutes,
  non-terminating, zero interop content -- spec Open Risk #9). If
  runtime/run throws (a malformed AWS_LAMBDA_RUNTIME_API, or the
  initial connection to the Runtime API sidecar failing), the
  exception propagates uncaught: jank prints its own error report to
  stderr, but the process then EXITS 0 -- verified directly (a trivial
  uncaught-throw probe), not assumed. This is worse for diagnosability
  than a non-zero exit would be: nothing in the exit status itself
  signals failure, so anything watching this process (a container
  orchestrator, Lambda's own supervisor) has to notice the *absence* of
  a Runtime API response instead. No runtime/post-init-error call
  happens either (there is no catch point left to call it from -- that
  fn stays public and unused in runtime.jank, ready to wire back in if
  a future jank release resolves the try/catch cost). Both effects
  together are a real, documented divergence from the Jolt sibling,
  which reports a clean init-error to the Lambda Runtime API AND still
  distinguishes success from failure via its own exit code."
  (:require [net.b12n.lambda-mvp.handler :as handler]
            [net.b12n.lambda-mvp.runtime :as runtime]))

(defn -main [& _args]
  (runtime/run handler/handle))
